1
00:04:37,241 --> 00:04:43,241
Sean Connery is James Bond 007

2
00:09:14,482 --> 00:09:20,482
Never Say Never Again is a adventure, action,
thriller film directed by Irvin Kershner.

3
00:13:51,724 --> 00:13:57,724
James Bond returns as the secret agent 007 to
battle the evil organization SPECTRE.

4
00:18:28,965 --> 00:18:34,965
Bond must defeat Largo, who has stolen two atomic
warheads for nuclear blackmail.

5
00:23:06,206 --> 00:23:12,206
But Bond has an ally in Largo's girlfriend, the willowy
Domino, who falls for Bond and seeks revenge.

6
00:27:43,448 --> 00:27:49,448
James Bond, played by Sean Connery.

7
00:32:20,689 --> 00:32:26,689
Maximilian Largo, played by Klaus Maria Brandauer.

8
00:36:57,931 --> 00:37:03,931
Ernst Stavro Blofeld, played by Max von Sydow.

9
00:41:35,172 --> 00:41:41,172
Fatima Blush, played by Barbara Carrera.

10
00:46:12,413 --> 00:46:18,413
Domino Petachi, played by Kim Basinger.

11
00:50:49,655 --> 00:50:55,655
Felix Leiter, played by Bernie Casey.

12
00:55:26,896 --> 00:55:32,896
Never Say Never Again is a 1983 spy film directed by Irvin Kershner.

13
01:00:04,137 --> 01:00:10,137
The film is based on the 1961 James Bond novel Thunderball by Ian Fleming, which in turn was
based on an original story by Kevin McClory, Jack Whittingham, and Fleming.

14
01:04:41,379 --> 01:04:47,379
The novel had been previously adapted as the 1965 film Thunderball.

15
01:09:18,620 --> 01:09:24,620
Never Say Never Again is the second and most recent James Bond film not to be produced by Eon Productions (the usual
producer of the Bond series) but instead by Jack Schwartzman's Taliafilm, and was distributed by Warner Bros.

16
01:13:55,862 --> 01:14:01,862
The film was executive produced by Kevin McClory, one of the
original writers of the Thunderball storyline.

17
01:18:33,103 --> 01:18:39,103
McClory had retained the filming rights of the novel
following a long legal battle dating from the 1960s.

18
01:23:10,344 --> 01:23:16,344
Sean Connery played the role of Bond for the seventh and final time, marking his
return to the character twelve years after Diamonds Are Forever (1971).

19
01:27:47,586 --> 01:27:53,586
The film's title is a reference to Connery's reported declaration
in 1971 that he would "never again" play that role.

20
01:32:24,827 --> 01:32:30,827
As Connery was 52 at the time of filming, the script makes frequent reference to Bond as aging and past his
prime – although Connery was three years younger than his Eon Productions replacement, Roger Moore.

21
01:37:02,068 --> 01:37:08,068
The storyline features Bond being reluctantly brought back into action to
investigate the theft of two nuclear weapons by SPECTRE.

22
01:41:39,310 --> 01:41:45,310
Filming locations included France, Spain, the Bahamas,
and Elstree Studios in the United Kingdom.

23
01:46:16,551 --> 01:46:22,551
Never Say Never Again was released by Warner Bros.

24
01:50:53,793 --> 01:50:59,793
on 7 October 1983, and opened to positive reviews, with critics praising
the cast's performances and Connery's return to the role.

25
01:55:31,034 --> 01:55:37,034
The film grossed $160 million at the box office, making it a commercial success, although it
earned less overall than the Eon-produced Octopussy, released earlier the same year.

26
02:00:08,275 --> 02:00:14,275
Retrospective reception to Never Say Never Again has been mixed, and it is
considered by several critics to be one of the weaker Bond films.

27
02:04:45,517 --> 02:04:51,517
The film explores themes of video game, spy, bahamas, stealing, scuba diving.

28
02:09:22,758 --> 02:09:28,758
The tone is intense.
